WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:05.340
With all the rewrites, test screenings, and
last-minute edits, it's amazing that any film

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:06.710
gets made at all.

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:10.170
But every so often, everything lines up just
perfectly.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:14.360
Bring together the right screenplay with the
right director, cast, and crew, and you just

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.510
might get a perfect film.

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:21.390
These movies are few and far between, but
when they come along, you know right away

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:24.200
you've found a flawless film.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:25.650
Mulholland Drive

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:30.890
Explaining the plot of Mulholland Drive is
like trying to describe a deep and disturbing

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:34.830
dream; it leaves you emotional and confused
in equal measure.

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:40.000
Director David Lynch uses the film to explore
the darker aspects of Hollywood and how often

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:42.800
our grand goals give way to despair.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:47.430
While you're sifting through all this dream
logic, Lynch keeps you glued to the screen

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:52.710
with mesmerizing sequences like Naomi Watts'
Betty turning standard movie audition dialogue

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:54.710
into something genuinely chilling:

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:58.210
"You're playing a dangerous game here.

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:04.685
If you're trying to blackmail me, it's not
going to work.

00:01:04.685 --> 00:01:09.850
It's no wonder that in 2016, the BBC named
this surrealist masterpiece the greatest film

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:12.530
of the 21st century.

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:17.370
"Il n'est pas d'orchestra.

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:17.980
It.

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:19.200
Is.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:21.720
An illusion.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.120
Manchester by the Sea

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:28.930
Written and directed by Kenneth Lonergan,
Manchester by the Sea tells the story of Lee

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:34.380
Chandler, an irritable janitor forced to return
to his hometown to look after his nephew following

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:35.770
the loss of his brother.

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:39.440
We don't want to give away too much of the
plot, but there's a reason that going home

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:42.270
is the last thing Lee wants to do.

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:47.280
Every sight and sound reminds him of something
truly tragic that happened years ago, an event

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:49.920
so horrible that he might never recover.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:54.979
Casey Affleck won an Oscar for his performance
as Lee, as did Lonergan for Best Original

00:01:54.979 --> 00:01:58.620
Screenplay, and the two truly deserved their
accolades.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:03.530
Every gesture Affleck makes, every line he
doesn't say, aches with pain.

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:08.090
And while the film is full of uncomfortably
comic moments, Manchester by the Sea grapples

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:11.410
with grief in a way that most movies wouldn't
dare.

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:16.170
It's a beautifully brutal film about living
with pain forever.

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:17.240
The Witch

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:21.610
One of the most disturbing horror movies in
recent memory, The Witch tells the story of

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:26.340
a Puritan family fighting for their lives
when a supernatural force attacks their isolated

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:27.580
farmhouse.

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:32.420
Director Robert Eggers spent four years studying
the Puritan lifestyle and reading firsthand

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:36.920
accounts of demonic possession in order to
write the script as accurately as possible.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:42.700
He also got super involved when it came to
sets and props: the costumes were hand-sewn,

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:46.220
and the furniture was built just like the
Puritans used to do it.

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:51.230
Eggers wanted everything perfect, and it certainly
shows in the actual movie; by paying so much

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:56.690
attention to the little details in every scene,
the film fully immerses viewers in the nightmarish

00:02:56.690 --> 00:02:57.690
setting.

00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:01.770
Even besides all the props and research, Eggers
managed to get incredible performances from

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:06.760
the child actors, and somehow found the scariest
goat to ever put on film.

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:10.980
"Black Phillip, antlers on his head! Black Phillip, Black Phillip..."

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:15.900
And if a movie can make livestock scary, you
know you've got an instant horror classic

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:17.709
on your hands.

00:03:17.709 --> 00:03:19.660
The Grand Budapest Hotel

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:24.459
In retrospect, it seems like Wes Anderson's
entire career was building up to The Grand

00:03:24.459 --> 00:03:25.739
Budapest Hotel.

00:03:25.739 --> 00:03:29.740
It's the perfect blend of all things Anderson,
from his meticulous world-building and witty

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:33.560
dialogue to the melancholy air that hovers
over all of his films.

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:38.300
The plot involves a lobby boy named Zero,
played by Tony Revolori, who comes to work

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:43.180
for the flamboyant and foul-mouthed Monsieur
Gustave at the Grand Budapest Hotel.

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:47.540
As with most Anderson movies, a plot summary
doesn't capture the unique wit and unmistakable

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:50.160
cinematography that makes Grand Budapest so
appealing.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:51.200
"What else?"

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:51.940
"Zero.

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:52.520
Confused."

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:53.160
"Zero.

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:54.160
Confused, indeed.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:55.920
The plot thickens, as they say.

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:59.870
Why, by the way, is it a soup metaphor?"

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:04.620
With a brilliant lead performance from Ralph
Fiennes as Gustave, The Grand Budapest Hotel

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:06.629
is a fanciful breath of fresh air.

00:04:06.629 --> 00:04:11.010
It's proof that style and substance can go
hand-in-hand.

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:12.010
Creed

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:17.459
Ryan Coogler's Creed isn't just a great Rocky
sequel, it's a great movie all on its own.

00:04:17.459 --> 00:04:22.460
Michael B. Jordan puts in a jaw-dropping performance
as Adonis Creed, the son of the late great

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:23.779
champ Apollo Creed.

00:04:23.779 --> 00:04:28.349
Adonis is eager to fight and desperate to
prove himself, and he turns to the legendary

00:04:28.349 --> 00:04:30.059
Rocky Balboa for help.

00:04:30.059 --> 00:04:35.780
As Adonis boxes his way to a title shot, director
Ryan Coogler builds on the franchise mythology

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:36.780
in exciting ways.

00:04:37.060 --> 00:04:41.080
And then there's the moment when Adonis goes
to battle the champ, and the famous Rocky

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:45.980
theme starts playing in the greatest nostalgia-fueled
moment of the last 20 years.

00:04:45.999 --> 00:04:51.191
Jordan is on his A-game here, giving us a
hero who's not a Rocky Balboa clone but a

00:04:51.191 --> 00:04:53.509
young man with his own issues and dreams.

00:04:53.509 --> 00:04:58.229
Creed was an underdog movie that defied everyone's
expectations, and it can hold its own as one

00:04:58.229 --> 00:05:01.229
of the greatest sequels of all time.

00:05:01.229 --> 00:05:02.430
Silence

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:08.210
In 1988, Martin Scorsese took a new look at
the Gospels with The Last Temptation of Christ,

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:13.129
and nearly 30 years later, Scorsese returned
to religion with Silence, a movie about a

00:05:13.129 --> 00:05:17.449
man's crisis of faith when it seems he's been
forsaken by God.

00:05:17.449 --> 00:05:22.569
The plot involves two Jesuit priests played
by Andrew Garfield and Adam Driver, who sneak

00:05:22.569 --> 00:05:27.009
into Japan to find a missing mentor who has
supposedly abandoned his faith.

00:05:27.009 --> 00:05:31.169
When Garfield's priest is captured, he finds
himself confronted with a difficult decision:

00:05:31.169 --> 00:05:35.750
Recant his faith and risk damnation, or watch
as his fellow Christians are tortured to death.

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:42.360
"Think of the suffering you have inflicted
on these people, all because of your selfish

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:44.189
dream of a Christian Japan."

00:05:44.189 --> 00:05:49.631
Silence is beautifully shot by Rodrigo Prieto,
who contrasts the beauty of Japan with the

00:05:49.631 --> 00:05:52.800
brutality of crucified men drowning in the
sea.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:58.080
Ultimately, Silence explores the nature of
faith: how it can inspire both hope and cruelty,

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:02.900
how it can rip apart lives and give others
the strength to soldier on.

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:04.240
Get Out

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:07.919
As Jordan Peele famously said on Colbert Report
The Late Show, Get Out is a documentary.

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:11.900
"You've submitted this to the Golden Globes
as a comedy."

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:16.220
"I submitted it as a documentary."

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:21.380
It's a fictional version of the horrors black
people experience every day in America.

00:06:21.389 --> 00:06:25.300
The story follows a young black photographer
named Chris, played wonderfully by Daniel

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:28.690
Kaluuya who goes to meet his white girlfriend's
family.

00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:32.659
From the moment he shows up at their estate,
everything seems off, and it's hard to tell

00:06:32.659 --> 00:06:36.699
if it's casual racism or something more overtly
sinister.

00:06:36.699 --> 00:06:41.039
This is Peele's directorial debut, but he
shows himself to be a master at drawing out

00:06:41.039 --> 00:06:46.080
the tension until everything explodes in a
bloody mess of jiu-jitsu, tea cups, and deer

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:47.080
antlers.

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:50.900
Although Peele is best-known for his comedic
work on Key and Peele, Get Out shows that

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:54.980
he's even better at terrifying an audience
than he was at making people laugh.

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:06.220
Eternal Sunshine of the Spotless Mind

00:07:06.229 --> 00:07:10.270
Michel Gondry is a director known for his
whimsical imagination.

00:07:10.270 --> 00:07:13.669
Charlie Kaufman is a screenwriter known for
his witty pessimism.

00:07:13.669 --> 00:07:17.749
When the two combined their cinematic powers
for Eternal Sunshine of the Spotless Mind,

00:07:17.749 --> 00:07:22.860
they created an all-time great romance that
took a long, hard look at the ugly side of

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:25.240
love and how relationships actually work.

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:29.219
"I've been thinking lately of how I was before
and how I am now and it's like he changed

00:07:29.219 --> 00:07:30.219
me.

00:07:30.219 --> 00:07:31.369
I feel like I'm always pissy now.

00:07:31.369 --> 00:07:34.729
I don't like myself when I'm with him, I don't
like myself anymore."

00:07:34.729 --> 00:07:40.330
This inventive romance skips through time
and memories, as Jim Carrey's Joel re-experiences

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:42.520
the memories of his failed relationship.

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:47.069
The film doesn't ignore the pain and pitfalls
of making a relationship work, and by examining

00:07:47.069 --> 00:07:51.429
the different stages of romance, this is a
movie that changes and grows with each and

00:07:51.429 --> 00:07:52.429
every viewing.

00:07:52.429 --> 00:07:54.819
"This is it Joel.

00:07:54.820 --> 00:07:57.820
It's going to be gone soon."

00:07:58.480 --> 00:07:59.700
"I know."

